WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:08.730
Welcome everyone. I'm Priya Natarajan,

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:13.095
an astrophysicist on the faculty in the Department of Astronomy and Physics at Yale.

00:00:13.095 --> 00:00:17.520
I also serve as the current Director of Yale's flagship interdisciplinary program,

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.325
the Franke Program in Science and the Humanities.

00:00:20.325 --> 00:00:22.830
I'm delighted to have you all attend to

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:26.370
these wonderful talk by a neuroscientist and author,

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:31.215
Mariano Sigman, as part of our ongoing mapping as knowing series.

00:00:31.215 --> 00:00:34.410
I want to first start by recognizing the generosity

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.530
of Mr. and Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:41.475
for endowing this program and many other activities at Yale.

00:00:41.475 --> 00:00:43.280
As a quick point of reminder,

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.350
since we are recording this event,

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:49.940
I would like to request you all to mute your videos and microphones.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:55.145
Please go ahead and submit your questions for our speaker through the Q&amp;A feature.

00:00:55.145 --> 00:01:00.700
Dr. Ty Kamp and I will relay these questions to him after the talk.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.435
Coming to our speaker today,

00:01:03.435 --> 00:01:07.730
Dr. Mariano Sigman is an eminent neuroscientist with

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:13.040
well over 150 publications and some of the most prestigious scientific journals.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.975
He's extremely research active.

00:01:14.975 --> 00:01:18.890
He's also very passionate about experimentation and has worked on

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:26.195
an unusually large set of collaborators that might sound really peculiar and intriguing;

00:01:26.195 --> 00:01:28.474
magicians, chess masters,

00:01:28.474 --> 00:01:31.350
musicians, athletes, and visual artists,

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.680
to bring his knowledge of neuroscience to

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.455
different aspects of human culture and activity,

00:01:36.455 --> 00:01:39.079
and apply it in different contexts.

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:44.645
He has participated several times in the TEDGlobal events in Vancouver.

00:01:44.645 --> 00:01:46.290
In one of these instances,

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:50.130
he participated with then [inaudible].

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:53.480
In his conferences, he teaches how the mind works and

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.650
what lessons we can learn about decision-making, leadership, team management,

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.545
personal development or creative skills,

00:02:00.545 --> 00:02:05.270
and his presentations always end up with a high level of

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:11.360
participation from the audience through experiments and gamification.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.665
Dr. Sigman obtained his degree in physics from the University of Buenos Aires,

00:02:15.665 --> 00:02:18.430
his doctorate in neuroscience in New York,

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:23.645
and he did a post-doc in cognitive sciences at the College de France in Paris.

00:02:23.645 --> 00:02:29.435
In 2016, he was awarded the PO11 medal from the Pontifical Academy of Science,

00:02:29.435 --> 00:02:31.680
which is a major science prize.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.980
Previous winners include Stephen Hawking and many notable Nobel Prize winners.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:43.160
Beyond the academy, his research has had practical implications for medicine,

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.445
for clinical treatments,

00:02:46.445 --> 00:02:51.860
for example, in the diagnosis of vegetative or autistic patients.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:54.790
He's also had a lot of impact in education,

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.560
with effective interventions to improve

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:02.215
decision-making and learning in schools throughout Latin America,

00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:05.300
and in art with a project at

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:08.750
the morphology of the look that has been exhibited now in New York,

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:10.370
Austria, Moscow, Mexico,

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:14.405
Japan, and was part of the 2017 Venice Biennale.

00:03:14.405 --> 00:03:18.500
He's also developed an extensive career in the public dissemination

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:22.220
of science that includes multitude of TV programs,

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.340
hundreds of articles published in different editorial media.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.450
He's also the author of the bestseller The Secret Life of the Mind.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:31.505
I highly recommend this book.

00:03:31.505 --> 00:03:33.125
Absolutely fell in love with it.

00:03:33.125 --> 00:03:35.480
It's just beautifully written.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.925
Before turning over the floor to him,

00:03:37.925 --> 00:03:42.340
I would just like to remind you all about our learning by doing project.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:44.510
Please do consider submitting your videos to

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:47.644
our collection of simple science experiments.

00:03:47.644 --> 00:03:51.560
Our final talk in the inference series will be given by

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:57.815
physicist Kyle Cranmer next week on December 15th at 3:00 PM.

00:03:57.815 --> 00:04:04.005
Without further ado, I would like to turn over to Mariano, welcome.

00:04:04.005 --> 00:04:07.205
We wish we could have welcomed you in person.

00:04:07.205 --> 00:04:11.000
We had been postponing get your talk,

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.890
hoping that we could get you in person,

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:15.650
but then ultimately decided that we didn't want to wait

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:19.040
any longer and that we're dying to hear you.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.145
Look forward to your talk. Thank you.

00:04:22.145 --> 00:04:28.530
Thank you so much Priya for such a warm introduction.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:32.480
I also would very much like to be able to be there.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:33.710
I went to New Haven.

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.600
When I was a child, my aunt,

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.740
she was a neurologist and she worked there at Yale actually.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.760
I went there as a child, and you know how we build

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.695
these child memories of these places as a bit mythical.

00:04:48.695 --> 00:04:53.380
For me, my aunt was a huge intellectual and a great neurologist.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:57.875
This place was the place where great scientists went.

00:04:57.875 --> 00:05:00.920
I've never gone back, interesting.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:06.200
I spent many years in the US in academia and I've been in many of the universities,

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:11.475
but I just brought these memory that for some reason I never came back to Yale.

00:05:11.475 --> 00:05:14.000
I very much hope will have the opportunity,

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.730
I would very much wish to go there again.

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:19.655
We look forward to your visit in person.

00:05:19.655 --> 00:05:25.115
This has brought me a very emotional memory from my childhood.

00:05:25.115 --> 00:05:28.990
I'm going to speak about decision-making.

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:34.250
This series builds on the relation between mapping and knowledge.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:37.115
I felt it would be interesting to begin,

00:05:37.115 --> 00:05:39.800
not so much with the science of decision,

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:45.590
but the other way around, how science has helped us make decisions in difficult domains.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:48.500
I think this has been often the role of science to

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:55.040
bring relevant data for having informed policies and informed decisions.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.160
This has happened in all fields of knowledge,

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.590
and of course this includes neuroscience and

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:04.940
especially the breach or the relation between neuroscience and medicine.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:10.820
Priya, you mentioned the work we've done on diagnosis of vegetative state patients.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:12.820
I think this is a very clear case,

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:15.770
where many years ago there was not

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:19.060
much that could be done because essentially we're looking at devoid,

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:23.630
and technology has changed in a way that now we have the capacity to

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:28.910
be able to infer thoughts of other people by looking at their brains.

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:32.180
This is a technology that may be flashy and interesting,

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:34.880
but it gets really relevant when this is the only way

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:37.870
that we have to communicate with some people.

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:41.240
This happens in three very peculiar domains.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:44.810
One is comatose patients or patients in

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:49.460
vegetative states where it looks like they are unconscious,

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:51.650
but we are really not sure if they're unconscious.

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:53.480
They do not express consciousness,

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:56.240
but of course that doesn't mean they do not have consciousness.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:00.440
Another example I'll be showing some data is on babies,

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:03.620
when they're previable and they cannot communicate their minds.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:08.810
As parents, we do all what we can to understand what they feel,

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:10.430
what they want, what they suffer,

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:11.840
what they desire,

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:14.930
and we do this exercise of complicated inference

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:18.350
where once again technology now can give us a good hand.

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:21.770
The third case, which I will not be speaking today, is dreams,

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:23.150
which also remains I think one of

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:27.365
the very interesting frontiers of knowledge in human neuroscience.

00:07:27.365 --> 00:07:30.420
Let me start with work done by Adrian Owen.

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.675
This is an extremely classic paper,

00:07:32.675 --> 00:07:34.790
has now 25 years.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:42.860
What you're seeing there is the brain image by fMRI of the group of people.

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:44.210
This is the average image,

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:45.650
and it's been thresholded,

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:48.424
so the pattern of course it would be much more complicated.

00:07:48.424 --> 00:07:51.530
But what you see on your left of the screen

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:55.745
here is what activates when someone imagines that plays tennis.

00:07:55.745 --> 00:07:57.900
This is nothing special about tennis,

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.170
it has to do with motor actions.

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:01.730
What you see here, lightened,

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:05.200
is a region in the brain called the supplementary motor area.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:08.405
This is a region that is involved with the coordination of movement.

00:08:08.405 --> 00:08:13.040
But even if we do not enact these movements and we just imagine them,

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:15.450
then this region also plays this enroll.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:20.150
It's like a music director that's directing the concert without its sounding.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:24.175
On the right you see how the brain of a person

00:08:24.175 --> 00:08:29.765
activates when they close their eyes and they imagine they are walking in their houses,

00:08:29.765 --> 00:08:31.970
or they're walking in the Yale campus,

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:34.610
or they're going from their house to their work.

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:37.760
We have very significant capacity.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:41.480
This comes from our ancestors broadens and all mammals to

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:45.230
navigate in space and their brain regions that are dedicated to this.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:49.820
I'm not so interested now in the brain science of these specific functions.

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:54.650
What I'm interested in is that now if you see these from an engineering perspective,

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:56.990
now what we have is a utility, a tool,

00:08:56.990 --> 00:09:00.080
a technology, that somehow we can tell very

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:05.550
rudimentary what the person is thinking just by looking at their brains.

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:09.260
Think whatever you want, either that you are playing tennis or that you

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:12.785
are walking in your house,

00:09:12.785 --> 00:09:14.210
then I could tell with

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:18.380
very high probability what is actually what you are thinking just looking at your brain.

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:21.080
Again, this is not very useful like we just ask you that.

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:23.495
But once again, we come to the vegetative state patients.

00:09:23.495 --> 00:09:25.430
We cannot ask them about their thoughts,

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:30.140
but somehow we can prompt them to make thoughts and then ask what happens.

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:37.400
Vegetative state patients they have been in the public scene for many years now.

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:38.900
The reason for this,

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:40.805
the first one is a purely medical reason,

00:09:40.805 --> 00:09:43.400
which is that 20 years ago,

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:45.140
these patients arrived in a coma to

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:47.960
a hospital and their likelihood of survival was very low.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:51.575
Now they survived, but they do so in a situation which once again,

00:09:51.575 --> 00:09:53.910
it's a huge mystery about what goes on,

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:55.715
and this is situation which of course leads to

00:09:55.715 --> 00:09:59.010
a lot of ethical problems, a lot of suffering.

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:04.850
Adrian Owen redid this experiment now with one patient that had

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:08.090
been five years completely uncommunicated and

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:11.650
with the idea that she was enabled to make explicit thoughts.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:14.475
What if she could but she could not communicate them?

00:10:14.475 --> 00:10:20.240
Could it be that reading their minds by MRI could detect that and that's what Adrian did?

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:23.150
What he found is that when he asked

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:26.920
this patient to imagine that she play tennis or that she moved,

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:30.590
apparently, she heard these message and she reacted and she

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:34.415
enacted this motion and the same thing with a spatial navigation.

00:10:34.415 --> 00:10:37.370
Of course, we do not know whether they're conscious about this thought,

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:38.705
it could be that they're unconscious.

00:10:38.705 --> 00:10:42.545
But at least we know that they understand something and they can react to that.

00:10:42.545 --> 00:10:44.180
Once you have this tool,

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:48.640
this is an engineering device that you can use now as a binary communication device.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:50.610
You can say whenever you want to say yes,

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:52.250
imagine that you play tennis,

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:53.480
whenever you want to say no,

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:57.515
then imagine that you're walking in your house.

00:10:57.515 --> 00:11:00.320
As any communication device you want to calibrate it,

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:01.940
and you want to calibrate with things that you know,

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:03.785
so you'll ask questions like, are you a woman?

00:11:03.785 --> 00:11:06.685
The answer is true, so you expect the answer to be yes.

00:11:06.685 --> 00:11:10.230
Are you younger than 23? The answer is no.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:12.420
Do you have three siblings?

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:13.770
The answer is no, and so on.

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:17.010
Once you have calibrated this communication device,

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:19.020
which is far from perfect,

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.330
but it's also much better than chance,

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:24.770
then you can start asking the questions that you would like to ask.

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:26.880
Are you suffering? What would you want?

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:29.870
What would you wish? What would you want us to do?

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:33.195
Then we go to a completely different sphere, the sphere of ethics,

00:11:33.195 --> 00:11:35.375
but it's one that's nurtured or that can be

00:11:35.375 --> 00:11:38.775
fueled by data that can be provided by science.

00:11:38.775 --> 00:11:41.775
This is of course a very important but also a very tough example.

00:11:41.775 --> 00:11:45.345
Let me now give you are much candid one, one which I think,

00:11:45.345 --> 00:11:47.970
we all, at least for all of us,

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:50.955
with our parents, we've grown through this experience.

00:11:50.955 --> 00:11:52.830
What you're seeing here is again,

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:54.840
the same strategy as I showed you before.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:59.625
First, the brain of a person in which the experiment is so to say, easy.

00:11:59.625 --> 00:12:03.255
This experiment is just the person listening to sentences.

00:12:03.255 --> 00:12:09.990
You can imagine these as what happens in your brain as you're listening me to speak.

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:13.755
What happens is that you see this pattern of incubations in these two big region,

00:12:13.755 --> 00:12:16.020
this is the auditory cortices and of course

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:18.720
we have the cortices activated when you hear sound.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:21.210
Also interestingly I can not go into details of this,

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:22.935
but you see even if this is an old paper,

00:12:22.935 --> 00:12:25.980
we were pushing the limits of temporal resolution.

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:30.750
You may know that if MRI is pretty decently good in spatial resolution,

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:34.820
you can get these maps which act like millimeter resolution,

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:37.245
but the temporal resolution is very low.

00:12:37.245 --> 00:12:40.410
Cognition happens in a fraction of a second and suddenly you

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:44.400
have a tool that actually had a resolution of a few seconds.

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:47.310
We were in the frontier of these and what you see here in

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:50.280
different colors is not the amount of activity,

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:52.950
which is what you typically see in an fMRI image,

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:54.210
but the timing of activity,

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:58.170
and you see going from, this is measured by the magnetic signal,

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:01.965
which is the MRI response and they measure the phase of

00:13:01.965 --> 00:13:11.085
this signal by convolving the response function and we could compute with these,

00:13:11.085 --> 00:13:14.375
or we could infer the temporality of the responses.

00:13:14.375 --> 00:13:15.525
What you see, once again,

00:13:15.525 --> 00:13:19.220
this is a very fast summary of something that could be an entire talk.

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:22.635
You see that there's an early activation in the cortex

00:13:22.635 --> 00:13:26.040
which projects to this region in the frontal left cortex,

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:28.695
very famous region you may have heard about,

00:13:28.695 --> 00:13:30.390
called the Broca region.

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:35.575
It's named Broca by French neurologist Broca.

00:13:35.575 --> 00:13:40.340
This region is known to be relevant and it's one of

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:43.720
the very highly specialized regions in the brain

00:13:43.720 --> 00:13:47.885
which is specialized for the articulation of language.

00:13:47.885 --> 00:13:50.430
If you have a fascia,

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:52.115
which means that you can understand language,

00:13:52.115 --> 00:13:53.625
but you cannot produce language,

00:13:53.625 --> 00:13:57.060
then typically you will have lesions in the Broca,

00:13:57.060 --> 00:14:00.960
people that have fascia have lesions in the Broca region.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:04.080
Now, if you've been following the logic of what I said,

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:07.845
there is something that may seem a bit strange because I'm saying this is the brain,

00:14:07.845 --> 00:14:10.845
the activations in brain of people listening to sentences,

00:14:10.845 --> 00:14:13.395
and yet they're activating one region which is

00:14:13.395 --> 00:14:16.140
known to be very specialized for the production of sentences,

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:18.645
which they're not making, they're just listening.

00:14:18.645 --> 00:14:26.235
As it happens with something that we've discovered or might not be entire field,

00:14:26.235 --> 00:14:29.130
is that there is a whole system of resonance called the

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:32.735
mirror system in the human brain and in the mammal brain,

00:14:32.735 --> 00:14:35.490
which is that essentially we internalize all the actions

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:38.625
you perceive by enacting them somehow.

00:14:38.625 --> 00:14:41.325
In a way when we hear two sentences,

00:14:41.325 --> 00:14:45.660
we're also producing internally and silently these sentences.

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:48.330
It's a little bit like I showed you in the previous slide when I said,

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:49.920
when you imagine that you're playing tennis,

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:51.390
you're not moving the muscles,

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:54.885
yet you're enacting the entire motor concert.

00:14:54.885 --> 00:14:59.880
Now the question is, what happens with young babies when they listen to sentences?

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:06.315
Because it's not that they will not produce languages that they can't.

00:15:06.315 --> 00:15:07.895
They have three months.

00:15:07.895 --> 00:15:11.615
They have to quadruple their lifetime to

00:15:11.615 --> 00:15:16.215
arrive to the moment in which they'll begin producing their first words and sentences.

00:15:16.215 --> 00:15:21.645
Is preverbal baby very far from the moment of

00:15:21.645 --> 00:15:27.285
the initiation of production of language, silently producing language.

00:15:27.285 --> 00:15:29.120
This was a philosophical question,

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:31.140
one that was not amenable to science.

00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:32.560
Now we have proxies.

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:34.260
We cannot completely answer this question,

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:36.365
but we have good ways of addressing this question,

00:15:36.365 --> 00:15:39.860
which is repeating this exact experiment in babies and asking whether,

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:42.495
is their Broca region already being activated,

00:15:42.495 --> 00:15:46.290
the region that actually works in the production of sentences.

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:49.650
Now what you have is a summary of three month old babies.

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:52.545
Once again, for those that have not experienced with babies,

00:15:52.545 --> 00:15:57.525
they are very far from producing language and yet on a way they're trying.

00:15:57.525 --> 00:16:00.330
I would like to present something which for me is very important here,

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:05.385
which is, this experiment I did just before I had my first child.

00:16:05.385 --> 00:16:08.905
I thought it was very important because I think in a way,

00:16:08.905 --> 00:16:12.090
a very essential thing of parenthood is that we try to detect

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:16.805
the struggles that our children may be undergoing, and we help them.

00:16:16.805 --> 00:16:19.815
If they are struggling with walking, we give them a hand.

00:16:19.815 --> 00:16:22.800
If they are struggling with eating, we'll help them on that,

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:28.815
but of course they're going through a million of struggles that we will never see.

00:16:28.815 --> 00:16:31.205
Because they're [inaudible], they are silent.

00:16:31.205 --> 00:16:33.060
I really like this experiment,

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:35.145
not only because of its scientific pertinence,

00:16:35.145 --> 00:16:37.170
because I thought it communicate me better.

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:39.610
It's what I like most of neuroscience,

00:16:39.610 --> 00:16:41.790
which is this capacity of getting

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:45.120
closer to the people that we would like eventually to get closer.

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:51.795
On a way, what this short story and I did exaggerate it of these result,

00:16:51.795 --> 00:16:54.915
is that in the same way the nine month old baby

00:16:54.915 --> 00:16:57.255
is trying to walk and they are struggling,

00:16:57.255 --> 00:16:58.580
a three month old baby,

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:00.895
although this is silent, they're trying to speak,

00:17:00.895 --> 00:17:02.915
they're trying to make their first words,

00:17:02.915 --> 00:17:04.170
so I accompany,

00:17:04.170 --> 00:17:07.760
I work with my son on this particular process.

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:14.860
This is just the way also on how science may be informing us on policies for education.

00:17:14.860 --> 00:17:16.295
Now not education in school,

00:17:16.295 --> 00:17:18.710
but maybe even more relevant for the patient,

00:17:18.710 --> 00:17:20.225
which is parental education.

00:17:20.225 --> 00:17:24.440
This is the first part of my talk and it has a different flavor, because once again,

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:27.810
it's not so much the neuroscience of decisions,

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:31.245
but it's the neuroscience that can help us,

00:17:31.245 --> 00:17:34.884
or it will give us information to make better decisions.

00:17:34.884 --> 00:17:40.085
Now I would like to go to a completely different space of decisions,

00:17:40.085 --> 00:17:43.985
which is the ones, we do not have this technology and this much information,

00:17:43.985 --> 00:17:46.130
the majority of the decisions we make, which is,

00:17:46.130 --> 00:17:49.500
somehow we have the information that we have, scarce information,

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:52.775
often not enough, sometimes even no information,

00:17:52.775 --> 00:17:56.135
and we have to make the best educated guess from what we have.

00:17:56.135 --> 00:17:58.740
Let me give you a first example of what

00:17:58.740 --> 00:18:02.375
we humans do when we are confronted with these type of decisions,

00:18:02.375 --> 00:18:04.930
then we'll go into the science of it,

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:08.070
and then we'll go on to how we can improve some of

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:12.690
the biases and some irrationalities that we tend to make these decisions.

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:14.490
This is a very classic experiment.

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:17.565
I love this experiment, I would like to do it with you on stage.

00:18:17.565 --> 00:18:22.580
It'd be much more interesting because we would sense it on a much better way.

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:25.020
It's a very classic demonstration.

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:29.525
The first end of scientific paper was published by Hubbard and Ramachandran,

00:18:29.525 --> 00:18:33.065
at UCSD, University of California and San Diego.

00:18:33.065 --> 00:18:35.420
The question is, these are two shapes,

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:39.490
one is bouba and the other one is kiki.

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:42.450
You know which one is which,

00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:45.720
but the task for you decision-makers is to tell me which is which.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:47.240
You can do this in the chat if you want.

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:48.720
You get that feeling,

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:50.495
you've got a public chat.

00:18:50.495 --> 00:18:52.170
If you do then you could do it.

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:57.585
Then you may have a public sense of the agreement that there isn't the response of these,

00:18:57.585 --> 00:19:03.840
typically 98 or 99 percent of the people agree that this is kiki and this is bouba.

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:06.680
This is quite interesting because it feels like completely

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:09.880
obvious as if it could not be in a different way.

00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:12.850
Yet, when we are asked why is the case,

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:15.285
it's just very hard because there is no reason for that.

00:19:15.285 --> 00:19:17.090
Some people do see a k here,

00:19:17.090 --> 00:19:19.260
but if I would say titi instead of kiki,

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:20.870
then it will be the same thing.

00:19:20.870 --> 00:19:25.605
The reason is known. The reason is that when we make sounds, we move the mouth.

00:19:25.605 --> 00:19:28.890
When we move the mouth, the mouth gives different shapes.

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:30.930
When I go, O, or U,

00:19:30.930 --> 00:19:33.845
those are called rounded vowels,

00:19:33.845 --> 00:19:38.760
so we think that the sounds correspond to rounded shapes.

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:45.300
Instead, when we make these sounds in which the [inaudible] contracts in a sharp way,

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:48.975
like [inaudible], then we imagine that this sounds

00:19:48.975 --> 00:19:53.895
are attacking consonants and are the ones that have sharp angles.

00:19:53.895 --> 00:19:57.225
When I say there is this correspondence,

00:19:57.225 --> 00:20:02.010
by no way that means that we are making these conscious logical inference.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:04.574
I'm saying, the brain is doing this calculation,

00:20:04.574 --> 00:20:06.830
we're completely unaware of it.

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:11.955
It biases our decisions in an extremely predictable and reliable way,

00:20:11.955 --> 00:20:15.285
yet we are completely unaware of this entire process.

00:20:15.285 --> 00:20:18.355
Now, this looks like a game and it doesn't look very important,

00:20:18.355 --> 00:20:22.505
but let me give you an example which I think it's much more relevant and you'll see

00:20:22.505 --> 00:20:26.840
how these same ideas start forging our decisions,

00:20:26.840 --> 00:20:31.680
from the main thing which we maybe would not like them to do so.

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:33.330
Here, this is, once again,

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:36.515
I mentioned Alexander Todorov, he's in Princeton.

00:20:36.515 --> 00:20:40.820
I think point to a specific paper because it's a collection of papers,

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:45.275
and this is my own demonstration from histories or east reference.

00:20:45.275 --> 00:20:47.960
If you can look at any of his many papers.

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:51.140
He's been showing how we make judgments about

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:54.875
people of all sorts from facial expressions.

00:20:54.875 --> 00:20:56.570
The way I like to frame this experiment,

00:20:56.570 --> 00:20:59.990
is imagine you have a suitcase with like the most important things in your life.

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:01.760
Actually an interesting term it is,

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:03.665
what would you put in this particular suitcase?

00:21:03.665 --> 00:21:07.985
What are the most important things in your life that you put in the small space.

00:21:07.985 --> 00:21:11.570
But imagine you look and so unfortunate strange reason doesn't matter here.

00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:16.385
You need to leave the suitcase with the most important things in your life.

00:21:16.385 --> 00:21:19.370
You're confronted with the situation,

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:20.600
you have to respond very rapidly,

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:23.735
and you have to decide there's these three persons here.

00:21:23.735 --> 00:21:25.790
You can think this as an investment problem.

00:21:25.790 --> 00:21:28.955
You have your money, you choose what bank to put it on.

00:21:28.955 --> 00:21:30.440
Each of these is one bank.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:32.795
This is like the technical way of thinking this experiment.

00:21:32.795 --> 00:21:35.015
The more real way would be you have something,

00:21:35.015 --> 00:21:37.415
a real thing and you have to give it to one of these three people.

00:21:37.415 --> 00:21:40.430
The question is, to whom you would give it to?

00:21:40.430 --> 00:21:44.405
Here it's not so such as sharp distribution as we saw before.

00:21:44.405 --> 00:21:46.370
But there's also very significant trend that's

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:49.130
observed in children and adults in America and Europe,

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:51.155
all over different cultures and so on.

00:21:51.155 --> 00:21:55.025
Which is that the majority of the people prefer to give it to the person in the center.

00:21:55.025 --> 00:21:57.500
The reasons why they do not give it.

00:21:57.500 --> 00:21:59.360
Some people will give it to the person on the right,

00:21:59.360 --> 00:22:00.785
and so people do person the left.

00:22:00.785 --> 00:22:07.175
But the reasoning beyond this particular decision is that the person on the right,

00:22:07.175 --> 00:22:09.515
we do not trust him so much,

00:22:09.515 --> 00:22:13.730
like we have the impression that we may come back and ask for a suitcase and he may go,

00:22:13.730 --> 00:22:16.685
"You know what, I like your suitcase and I will not give it back to you".

00:22:16.685 --> 00:22:21.545
We do not want to go through this confrontation with this particular person.

00:22:21.545 --> 00:22:23.750
The reason why the majority of the people do not

00:22:23.750 --> 00:22:25.925
give it to the person on the left is very different.

00:22:25.925 --> 00:22:28.025
Is we think he's like so naive,

00:22:28.025 --> 00:22:29.630
that he will give it back to us,

00:22:29.630 --> 00:22:31.070
or maybe he would give it to anyone,

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:32.090
and he will lose it,

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:33.740
or he'll be in this case.

00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:36.395
Now, the interesting thing is, I mean,

00:22:36.395 --> 00:22:39.620
I think you get the point that the majority of us do

00:22:39.620 --> 00:22:42.635
make these very fast and rapid inferences,

00:22:42.635 --> 00:22:46.430
and the interesting point is that we have no idea about these people.

00:22:46.430 --> 00:22:51.920
We have not a single piece of evidence about how they would behave.

00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:55.460
Yet, we make conclusions and conclusions in which

00:22:55.460 --> 00:22:59.540
the majority of the people is highly confident,

00:22:59.540 --> 00:23:04.910
really trust this impression of what the right action should be.

00:23:04.910 --> 00:23:07.160
It could be. It's possible,

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:10.535
it's not impossible that the faces of actually you can see also that this,

00:23:10.535 --> 00:23:13.010
it's the same person that has been only changed

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:15.740
the curvature of the mouth and the eyebrows and so on.

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:18.650
Now, it could be that there is a correlation

00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:21.635
between people's faces and people's attitudes.

00:23:21.635 --> 00:23:23.810
I mean, this is not impossible, but it's not true.

00:23:23.810 --> 00:23:25.865
I mean, the Nobel Prize Kaniban,

00:23:25.865 --> 00:23:29.285
Austin Princeton has shown

00:23:29.285 --> 00:23:32.600
many times that these correlations actually do not hold in any way.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:38.120
The conclusions that we make so strongly by people's appearances are completely

00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:43.985
ungrounded and they leave it to completely wrong decisions in which we deeply trust.

00:23:43.985 --> 00:23:49.610
Now, the example of [inaudible] was again,

00:23:49.610 --> 00:23:51.815
this is very relevant because for instance,

00:23:51.815 --> 00:23:55.910
it's known that the face of a person shapes elections.

00:23:55.910 --> 00:24:02.555
If I were to show you two candidates of a completely unknown county of Australia,

00:24:02.555 --> 00:24:04.430
and ask you who you think will win.

00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:08.075
You'll be 55 or 60 percent,

00:24:08.075 --> 00:24:11.540
and what this means is that only by the information in

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:15.440
the face is what the voters are using to make their choices.

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:18.110
Because you have no idea of who is a Democrat,

00:24:18.110 --> 00:24:19.805
who's a Republican, who's progresses,

00:24:19.805 --> 00:24:22.445
who is conservative, who has a good history.

00:24:22.445 --> 00:24:24.980
No information at all, only with information of the face.

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:28.220
You can make this sort of conclusion.

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:30.770
Also, it's very well known in human resources.

00:24:30.770 --> 00:24:34.580
The majority of the people decide

00:24:34.580 --> 00:24:38.585
who to hire on the first 500 milliseconds of an interview.

00:24:38.585 --> 00:24:45.890
Then they spend 20 minutes only to justify the decision that they have already made.

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:48.590
So I've given you two examples.

00:24:48.590 --> 00:24:50.000
I could give you 20.

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:53.190
I mean, there are millions of them. There are funny examples.

00:24:55.060 --> 00:25:00.360
What's consistent about these examples is how we are very stereotypical.

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:01.810
People call it irrational.

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:03.940
I don't think it's irrational because there is rationality,

00:25:03.940 --> 00:25:07.135
only that we are doing rationality on the wrong ground.

00:25:07.135 --> 00:25:08.200
On the wrong evidence,

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:10.270
and on the wrong information.

00:25:10.270 --> 00:25:13.720
But there's a smart inference process.

00:25:13.720 --> 00:25:18.250
But that takes us with very high confidence to a wrong conclusions.

00:25:18.250 --> 00:25:22.585
Now what I would like to do is to tell you how this process builds.

00:25:22.585 --> 00:25:25.095
How we actually build these intuitions.

00:25:25.095 --> 00:25:30.050
Once again, I'm bringing you some classes, A or B field.

00:25:30.050 --> 00:25:33.980
This is an experiment that you see now has 20 something years.

00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:36.890
It was done by Befarer and Benacio very,

00:25:36.890 --> 00:25:38.415
very famous experiment,

00:25:38.415 --> 00:25:42.080
in which they tried to understand how we

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:46.415
construct these hunches and these conscious intuitions.

00:25:46.415 --> 00:25:47.900
I think it's a very beautiful experiment.

00:25:47.900 --> 00:25:50.090
Let me tell you exactly how it goes.

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:52.715
It's also an investment experiment.

00:25:52.715 --> 00:25:55.680
The way it goes is that people are given two decks,

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:57.490
and these decks have payoffs.

00:25:57.490 --> 00:25:59.470
These payoffs can be either positive or negative.

00:25:59.470 --> 00:26:00.985
You can win money or it can lose money.

00:26:00.985 --> 00:26:07.100
It's a simulation of you have to choose between two portfolios in stocks.

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:10.160
You can choose between them and sometimes you win, sometimes you lose.

00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:13.115
You'll try to do some statistic or learning to understand,

00:26:13.115 --> 00:26:16.865
on average what's the right policy, and that's what people do.

00:26:16.865 --> 00:26:18.395
They choose from one,

00:26:18.395 --> 00:26:19.895
they win. So they persist.

00:26:19.895 --> 00:26:21.740
This is very typical then they win again,

00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:25.400
they persist, we've made this inference, and we've won,

00:26:25.400 --> 00:26:28.190
there's no reason to explore which is not optimal,

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:29.720
but that's what people do with your winning,

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:31.730
here you're very unlikely to go elsewhere.

00:26:31.730 --> 00:26:33.140
Eventually you lose,

00:26:33.140 --> 00:26:35.720
and then people do the contrary, they overswitch.

00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:38.420
They go to the other deck and they play here

00:26:38.420 --> 00:26:41.360
for a little while and then they lose and they switch and so on.

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:44.675
After an hour of play or something,

00:26:44.675 --> 00:26:46.700
they reached this moment here,

00:26:46.700 --> 00:26:48.080
which is the conceptual moment.

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:53.165
It happens that one of the decks has a highest average of return than the other one.

00:26:53.165 --> 00:26:55.100
This is what's called the correct choice,

00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:56.795
because it's the one that on average,

00:26:56.795 --> 00:26:59.075
if you continue playing on this,

00:26:59.075 --> 00:27:02.780
regardless of the stochasticity which you cannot control.

00:27:02.780 --> 00:27:06.665
It is the policy that will give you a higher expected value.

00:27:06.665 --> 00:27:08.600
Indian of these mini-trials,

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:11.090
people choose from the right bank,

00:27:11.090 --> 00:27:13.250
and when you ask them why they are doing so,

00:27:13.250 --> 00:27:15.425
they go up, they explain exactly what I just saw.

00:27:15.425 --> 00:27:17.000
Well, you know, you win,

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:19.325
you lose, but on average these one is better than the other ones.

00:27:19.325 --> 00:27:23.105
They have understanding and they understood the problem perfectly well.

00:27:23.105 --> 00:27:27.155
What is interesting is how is the path to this particular moment.

00:27:27.155 --> 00:27:29.915
Some trials before that,

00:27:29.915 --> 00:27:33.005
there is a moment that the mass equals the hunch moment,

00:27:33.005 --> 00:27:37.310
which is the following; people play from the right deck,

00:27:37.310 --> 00:27:39.380
and if you ask them what they are doing.

00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:42.440
They say, "Well, I'm playing from this one, I'm choosing from the right".

00:27:42.440 --> 00:27:44.720
Why? I don't know,

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:48.710
I have this feeling that's the right thing to do, but I cannot tell you.

00:27:48.710 --> 00:27:51.710
I mean, it's just a bad feeling I have had.

00:27:51.710 --> 00:27:53.660
There you understand the genesis of the bad feelings.

00:27:53.660 --> 00:27:54.950
They have nothing esoteric,

00:27:54.950 --> 00:27:56.974
nothing mystical, nothing irrational.

00:27:56.974 --> 00:28:00.590
It's a full-blown statistical learning process,

00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:03.215
which yet has not come into works.

00:28:03.215 --> 00:28:06.260
The brain has learned something and there's no mystery as to

00:28:06.260 --> 00:28:09.500
why has learned something because it can be statistical information to learn it.

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:12.710
But this knowledge, as the majority of knowledge we have,

00:28:12.710 --> 00:28:16.370
cannot yet be explained in a way that we can teach it in a lecture.

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:19.250
It's not explicit knowledge, is implicit knowledge.

00:28:19.250 --> 00:28:22.070
We'd like to give you a feel of what intuitions are.

00:28:22.070 --> 00:28:26.240
Intuitions are essentially a form of learning.

00:28:26.240 --> 00:28:28.760
Statistical learning of different domains

00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:30.890
and different disciplines, different scenarios,

00:28:30.890 --> 00:28:32.525
and different portions of the universe,

00:28:32.525 --> 00:28:37.115
which yet we have not made to explicit knowledge.

00:28:37.115 --> 00:28:39.200
Now there is an even more interesting phase,

00:28:39.200 --> 00:28:43.310
which is what Damasio called the pre-hand, which is the following.

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:45.995
In this phase, people as you see, are choosing,

00:28:45.995 --> 00:28:50.885
on average a little bit more from the right deck than from the wrong deck.

00:28:50.885 --> 00:28:53.315
But they don't even know they're doing that.

00:28:53.315 --> 00:28:55.160
If you ask them what they are doing,

00:28:55.160 --> 00:28:57.020
they will tell you I'm just picking a chance,

00:28:57.020 --> 00:28:59.105
I'm going one and the other one I'm trying to learn.

00:28:59.105 --> 00:29:00.230
There's no information,

00:29:00.230 --> 00:29:02.315
no structure. That's not what they're doing.

00:29:02.315 --> 00:29:03.980
They're picking more from the right.

00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:06.035
Brain has this information.

00:29:06.035 --> 00:29:12.080
This has not yet in terms of body expressions of wrong and right decision.

00:29:12.080 --> 00:29:14.104
There is not even this information,

00:29:14.104 --> 00:29:17.600
but they do have this information which

