00:42:47.130 --> 00:42:50.445
hope was convincing that we make these stereotype errors,

00:42:50.445 --> 00:42:53.730
and their problem precisely because they are stereotyped,

00:42:53.730 --> 00:42:55.530
we're not aware of them.

00:42:55.530 --> 00:42:57.225
How can we be aware of them?

00:42:57.225 --> 00:43:00.300
In the moment you have to explain this to someone else.

00:43:00.300 --> 00:43:04.215
We asked, what is it that people are doing when they get together?

00:43:04.215 --> 00:43:06.960
What we saw, the first thing is that,

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:08.685
if you take all the results,

00:43:08.685 --> 00:43:09.810
imagine that you have a group,

00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:11.370
like i the number of cities,

00:43:11.370 --> 00:43:15.355
and there was like a 400 and 480 and someone responded 75.

00:43:15.355 --> 00:43:18.245
The first thing you could do is just the average and this is good enough.

00:43:18.245 --> 00:43:19.490
If you were to do this,

00:43:19.490 --> 00:43:20.990
then you would get the blue curve.

00:43:20.990 --> 00:43:24.185
But the fact that the black curve is better than the blue curve,

00:43:24.185 --> 00:43:27.260
is showing that the groups are doing something better than average.

00:43:27.260 --> 00:43:30.810
This is interesting because we did not give groups any policy,

00:43:30.810 --> 00:43:34.930
and yet it turns out that groups are smarter than they would be

00:43:34.930 --> 00:43:39.460
just by pulling their information together in the most classical way.

00:43:39.460 --> 00:43:42.130
If you look at all possible mathematical models,

00:43:42.130 --> 00:43:46.550
what you get is that what people do is optimal statistically,

00:43:46.550 --> 00:43:50.630
which is that they tend to combine information of everybody,

00:43:50.630 --> 00:43:54.335
but they do so on a way that's weighted by the confidence.

00:43:54.335 --> 00:43:58.850
This is a weighted average by which you give more value to the person that claims

00:43:58.850 --> 00:44:03.410
to know more and less value to the person that actually claims to know less.

00:44:03.410 --> 00:44:06.740
Second typical robust statistical theme,

00:44:06.740 --> 00:44:09.530
is to identify where this is properly rated,

00:44:09.530 --> 00:44:12.440
you try to see if the people that have high confidence,

00:44:12.440 --> 00:44:14.120
and this I did not have enough time to dwell on.

00:44:14.120 --> 00:44:16.040
We can go on to statistics questions are

00:44:16.040 --> 00:44:18.440
impostors and you weigh people do that

00:44:18.440 --> 00:44:20.930
is because they have high confidence in all questions.

00:44:20.930 --> 00:44:23.420
If someone who claims to have high confidence in your questions,

00:44:23.420 --> 00:44:26.090
then the group do not use this confidence information.

00:44:26.090 --> 00:44:28.535
But if they do so on some questions but not others,

00:44:28.535 --> 00:44:30.290
then they begin to use information.

00:44:30.290 --> 00:44:32.855
Finally, of course from this average they feed their outlier.

00:44:32.855 --> 00:44:35.555
If someone would say that they're like

00:44:35.555 --> 00:44:41.720
three million cities with more than one million people,

00:44:41.720 --> 00:44:47.060
then groups would say we did not pick this information the average because [inaudible].

00:44:47.060 --> 00:44:48.425
This is interesting enough.

00:44:48.425 --> 00:44:50.180
But if I look at this information here,

00:44:50.180 --> 00:44:52.700
but if I were to put the confidence weighted average,

00:44:52.700 --> 00:44:54.980
it will be somewhere between the blue line and

00:44:54.980 --> 00:44:57.950
the black line but people do even better than

00:44:57.950 --> 00:45:03.290
the best mathematical model to combine the results.

00:45:03.290 --> 00:45:06.155
The reason is that people do not combine the results.

00:45:06.155 --> 00:45:11.300
They combine the methods by which they reached those results.

00:45:11.300 --> 00:45:12.875
They discuss, well,

00:45:12.875 --> 00:45:14.450
how did you think this problem?

00:45:14.450 --> 00:45:15.680
and then they go, yeah,

00:45:15.680 --> 00:45:18.110
but you made an error because looking Europe,

00:45:18.110 --> 00:45:22.655
cities are not so populated and they begin questioning

00:45:22.655 --> 00:45:27.715
actually the arguments and the reasons that what kind of hidden in the unconscious.

00:45:27.715 --> 00:45:31.045
That becomes just monitoring process

00:45:31.045 --> 00:45:35.275
by which you make explicit all the arguments that lead to this decision.

00:45:35.275 --> 00:45:38.309
Very simple, but very powerful,

00:45:38.309 --> 00:45:41.660
is a healthy way of making good decisions and avoiding

00:45:41.660 --> 00:45:43.700
these biases is just whenever you have to

00:45:43.700 --> 00:45:46.415
make an important decision you look for someone else,

00:45:46.415 --> 00:45:49.865
can not be 300 people because that's not the good conversation,

00:45:49.865 --> 00:45:52.445
just pick two or three or four people.

00:45:52.445 --> 00:45:56.600
Ideally, they have to think different from you so that you will be forced to

00:45:56.600 --> 00:46:01.280
explain them the reasonings that led you to this particular choice.

00:46:01.280 --> 00:46:06.035
In doing so, you are very likely to find errors than would have been

00:46:06.035 --> 00:46:08.285
remained completely unconscious if you did not

00:46:08.285 --> 00:46:11.090
go through this particular filtering process.

00:46:11.090 --> 00:46:15.350
I'd like to tell you something else because I mentioned the idea I could finish here,

00:46:15.350 --> 00:46:16.850
but I would like to tell you one more experiment.

00:46:16.850 --> 00:46:18.950
This is the one we did with a dialect.

00:46:18.950 --> 00:46:24.620
We also did it in a group with 10,000 people and then with Dan Ariely.

00:46:24.620 --> 00:46:27.830
Here the question was very similar to what I just presented.

00:46:27.830 --> 00:46:31.985
But it was not on questions that have a ground truth,

00:46:31.985 --> 00:46:35.090
and on questions that actually are more in

00:46:35.090 --> 00:46:38.600
the domain of morality or ideology or politics.

00:46:38.600 --> 00:46:42.095
Those things that we just strongly believe they're right or wrong.

00:46:42.095 --> 00:46:44.780
Those things that as I said, are very polarized.

00:46:44.780 --> 00:46:48.770
Some people have very strong arguments that made them believe for A,

00:46:48.770 --> 00:46:52.145
and some people have very strong arguments that make them for B.

00:46:52.145 --> 00:46:54.005
Apparently there's no way,

00:46:54.005 --> 00:46:56.345
even less way of putting this together.

00:46:56.345 --> 00:46:58.685
I would like to make sure the claim that this is not

00:46:58.685 --> 00:47:04.880
an inevitable phase of human dialogue and human interactions.

00:47:04.880 --> 00:47:11.704
It's just a consequence of conversations happening in the wrong space,

00:47:11.704 --> 00:47:15.260
which is typically space of social networks and

00:47:15.260 --> 00:47:19.280
the space of a lot of people shouting at the same time.

00:47:19.280 --> 00:47:20.945
I want to make the claim,

00:47:20.945 --> 00:47:25.280
scientifically that when people get together in small groups,

00:47:25.280 --> 00:47:27.620
then they become more [inaudible],

00:47:27.620 --> 00:47:32.690
they become much more malleable and much more open than they actually think they are.

00:47:32.690 --> 00:47:35.855
The way we did this experiment is we had dilemma.

00:47:35.855 --> 00:47:37.670
This is just one dilemma.

00:47:37.670 --> 00:47:41.270
You'll see they're uncomfortable dilemma you can read them.

00:47:41.270 --> 00:47:49.130
A Company offers a service that takes a fertilized egg and produces millions of embryos,

00:47:49.130 --> 00:47:52.610
with slight genetic variation this allows parents to have child

00:47:52.610 --> 00:47:56.285
that shares their combined genomes at the same time selecting their child's height,

00:47:56.285 --> 00:47:58.745
eye-color, intelligence, social competence,

00:47:58.745 --> 00:48:00.800
and other non-health related issue.

00:48:00.800 --> 00:48:03.470
What the company does is and

00:48:03.470 --> 00:48:07.175
then you can say it's right or it's wrong according to your beliefs,

00:48:07.175 --> 00:48:10.085
each one has a position people feel strongly about.

00:48:10.085 --> 00:48:12.830
We had some that were like even much more strongly,

00:48:12.830 --> 00:48:18.840
especially when they touch very practical thing and very actual things of human ideology.

00:48:18.840 --> 00:48:22.150
Of course not interested in any moral conversation of this.

00:48:22.150 --> 00:48:25.270
I'm just interested in what goes on in

00:48:25.270 --> 00:48:29.440
the decision process that makes us conclude what we conclude.

00:48:29.440 --> 00:48:31.920
Each person would make a different conclusion.

00:48:31.920 --> 00:48:38.120
The idea that we may be more malleable and that all of us maybe not seeing as much of

00:48:38.120 --> 00:48:40.070
the space of argumentation is that we

00:48:40.070 --> 00:48:44.105
think when we get together with someone that feels differently,

00:48:44.105 --> 00:48:46.430
then we may change more than we thought.

00:48:46.430 --> 00:48:47.855
This is what I will try to show you.

00:48:47.855 --> 00:48:49.850
I got three slides to make this.

00:48:49.850 --> 00:48:53.180
This was the distribution of this particular population.

00:48:53.180 --> 00:48:55.550
This was A, this is nothing universal.

00:48:55.550 --> 00:48:58.655
It will change with different people of different ages,

00:48:58.655 --> 00:49:01.175
people in different places, it's just a norm.

00:49:01.175 --> 00:49:05.945
You see, a lot of people where very zero means is completely wrong.

00:49:05.945 --> 00:49:08.420
You see this here that means it's completely right.

00:49:08.420 --> 00:49:10.880
You see that in the same space with

00:49:10.880 --> 00:49:13.580
people that more or less have the same background and so on.

00:49:13.580 --> 00:49:17.675
You have this diversity of someone sitting next to another one,

00:49:17.675 --> 00:49:19.220
that one thinks that it's completely

00:49:19.220 --> 00:49:21.455
wrong and the other one thinks it's completely right.

00:49:21.455 --> 00:49:25.910
The question is, we will put these people together in a group, is it?

00:49:25.910 --> 00:49:29.795
and we will ask them to think like they are in an ethical committee,

00:49:29.795 --> 00:49:33.830
and their job is to come up with a consent number for these.

00:49:33.830 --> 00:49:35.735
Like saying, well, we give it a four,

00:49:35.735 --> 00:49:37.670
we give it a six like it's not completely right,

00:49:37.670 --> 00:49:43.100
but it's an important thing is it has to be unanimous. It's three people.

00:49:43.100 --> 00:49:46.100
The three of them have to sign the paper and say,

00:49:46.100 --> 00:49:48.860
I think this is the right thing to do.

00:49:48.860 --> 00:49:50.540
They all have the option of saying, no,

00:49:50.540 --> 00:49:53.660
I do not want to sign this and they have this opt out.

00:49:53.660 --> 00:49:55.220
The question we're asking here,

00:49:55.220 --> 00:49:59.120
it's not so much to what consensus they reach the question before,

00:49:59.120 --> 00:50:00.320
although we also asked this question,

00:50:00.320 --> 00:50:02.705
but the first interesting question here is,

00:50:02.705 --> 00:50:05.825
what is the likelihood that they will reach consensus?

00:50:05.825 --> 00:50:08.600
For this, something that you have to know,

00:50:08.600 --> 00:50:11.510
which is important is that people in

00:50:11.510 --> 00:50:15.050
the same way that they distribute their opinions on whether it's right or wrong,

00:50:15.050 --> 00:50:18.500
they also distribute their confidence in a way that is predictable,

00:50:18.500 --> 00:50:20.480
which is that people that are in the extremes,

00:50:20.480 --> 00:50:25.025
they're very short position and the people in the middle which we call the grades,

00:50:25.025 --> 00:50:26.990
they doubt more, they are not sure,

00:50:26.990 --> 00:50:29.315
and so typically their confidence is way lower.

00:50:29.315 --> 00:50:32.960
But you may see here something which actually is still the dilemma,

00:50:32.960 --> 00:50:36.350
it's small, but it's still which is the people in the center in the very center,

00:50:36.350 --> 00:50:37.880
you have higher confidence.

00:50:37.880 --> 00:50:40.655
This is because there is a bimodal distribution

00:50:40.655 --> 00:50:43.730
in which there is a population of people that are in the center,

00:50:43.730 --> 00:50:46.325
but have very high confidence.

00:50:46.325 --> 00:50:49.145
We call these people the high confident grades.

00:50:49.145 --> 00:50:54.905
Those are the people that think this dilemma has real reasons for being right,

00:50:54.905 --> 00:50:56.660
real reasons for being wrong.

00:50:56.660 --> 00:51:00.635
I'm really sure that the right thing to do is to stand

00:51:00.635 --> 00:51:05.870
somewhere in a central place in the space of options.

00:51:05.870 --> 00:51:09.290
Here you have the distribution of confidence for people.

00:51:09.290 --> 00:51:10.970
When people have medium confidence on this,

00:51:10.970 --> 00:51:13.070
these are the high confident grades.

00:51:13.070 --> 00:51:15.980
Now my last slide,

00:51:15.980 --> 00:51:19.220
what happens when we put these A people together?

00:51:19.220 --> 00:51:21.005
and of course the likelihood.

00:51:21.005 --> 00:51:24.435
The question is, what is the likelihood that they will reach a consensus?

00:51:24.435 --> 00:51:29.620
Of course, if we do this as a function of their initial disagreement, and of course,

00:51:29.620 --> 00:51:31.090
if the initial disagreement is zero,

00:51:31.090 --> 00:51:32.740
meaning that they had the same ideas,

00:51:32.740 --> 00:51:34.990
then they naturally reach reachable census.

00:51:34.990 --> 00:51:36.380
It's very simple. You think it's wrong,

00:51:36.380 --> 00:51:37.790
I think it's wrong, or you think it's right,

00:51:37.790 --> 00:51:41.095
I think it's right, we just sign the paper and we go out very rapidly.

00:51:41.095 --> 00:51:43.575
Of course, as there is more difference,

00:51:43.575 --> 00:51:45.950
this decreases. That's natural.

00:51:46.890 --> 00:51:52.405
It's highly expected. The real surprise is that this value,

00:51:52.405 --> 00:51:54.265
even with very high disagreement,

00:51:54.265 --> 00:51:55.885
is close to 50 percent.

00:51:55.885 --> 00:51:57.045
It varies in the question,

00:51:57.045 --> 00:51:59.685
goes from 20-60 percent,

00:51:59.685 --> 00:52:05.655
but it's always one order of magnitude higher than what people think,

00:52:05.655 --> 00:52:06.960
if we ask them,

00:52:06.960 --> 00:52:10.135
is the likelihood that they will reach an agreement.

00:52:10.135 --> 00:52:13.140
If I were to run this experimentally,

00:52:13.140 --> 00:52:15.280
which we've done, and ask people,

00:52:15.280 --> 00:52:17.980
what do you think is the likelihood that if I put someone that thinks

00:52:17.980 --> 00:52:20.740
this is completely right with someone that this is completely wrong,

00:52:20.740 --> 00:52:23.140
[inaudible] 100 of this group,

00:52:23.140 --> 00:52:24.505
how many will reach agreement?

00:52:24.505 --> 00:52:27.375
People who say one, two, sometimes zero.

00:52:27.375 --> 00:52:30.905
Reality is that in the right condition,

00:52:30.905 --> 00:52:36.290
people speaking and listening in an open situation and not too many people shouting,

00:52:36.290 --> 00:52:38.850
then the likelihood of getting together,

00:52:38.850 --> 00:52:42.490
of agreeing to a consensus and to depolarizing,

00:52:42.490 --> 00:52:44.765
is much higher than what people would expect.

00:52:44.765 --> 00:52:48.540
Once again, conversations converge to rationality

00:52:48.540 --> 00:52:52.945
and not to irrationality when they are in their natural habitat, in their right domain.

00:52:52.945 --> 00:52:55.135
The last thing is, okay,

00:52:55.135 --> 00:52:57.085
50 percent of the groups, but what is it?

00:52:57.085 --> 00:52:59.370
Why some do and some don't?

00:52:59.370 --> 00:53:02.805
The answer to this, the most important factor,

00:53:02.805 --> 00:53:05.365
is whether there is a high-confident gray.

00:53:05.365 --> 00:53:10.150
If there's someone in the group that can speak to both parties and say,

00:53:10.150 --> 00:53:12.430
you're right, and you're also right,

00:53:12.430 --> 00:53:15.625
and look, what he's or what she's saying may have some reason.

00:53:15.625 --> 00:53:21.305
There's someone that understands that it's not idiots against intelligents,

00:53:21.305 --> 00:53:23.600
in whatever side you want to see the balance going,

00:53:23.600 --> 00:53:25.560
but people with arguments from both sides,

00:53:25.560 --> 00:53:30.255
then the likelihood of convergence increases substantially.

00:53:30.255 --> 00:53:31.670
You see not a huge effect.

00:53:31.670 --> 00:53:36.460
It's not like they guarantee that there is likelihood of consensus,

00:53:36.460 --> 00:53:39.150
but they increase [inaudible] substantially.

00:53:39.150 --> 00:53:43.240
This is a laboratory experiment,

00:53:43.240 --> 00:53:46.040
but many people have done these experiments in the grounds.

00:53:46.040 --> 00:53:48.730
I think the most interesting experiments are done in the frontier

00:53:48.730 --> 00:53:52.675
between Israel and Palestine with the exact same results.

00:53:52.675 --> 00:53:56.320
In the moment of most ferocious fights,

00:53:56.320 --> 00:54:00.610
the situation where you think there's no way these people will understand,

00:54:00.610 --> 00:54:02.410
you put the most radicals from one place and

00:54:02.410 --> 00:54:04.750
the most radicals from the other place and you make them talk.

00:54:04.750 --> 00:54:06.880
If there's a high-confident gray that

00:54:06.880 --> 00:54:09.570
actually gives this perspective of malleability behavior that,

00:54:09.570 --> 00:54:12.490
you know what, take out of your mind that your people will not change,

00:54:12.490 --> 00:54:14.980
they may change because you're thinking this way,

00:54:14.980 --> 00:54:20.065
then groups now in the real ground,

00:54:20.065 --> 00:54:22.975
not in this moral-simulated experiments,

00:54:22.975 --> 00:54:25.805
are much more likely to reach consensus.

00:54:25.805 --> 00:54:27.105
Not only that, but also,

00:54:27.105 --> 00:54:32.185
if you measure their likelihood of participating together in collaborative work,

00:54:32.185 --> 00:54:33.955
in working in the same enterprises,

00:54:33.955 --> 00:54:36.475
future behavior of these people,

00:54:36.475 --> 00:54:38.460
it's a complete game changer.

00:54:38.460 --> 00:54:39.800
I think that there's something interesting.

00:54:39.800 --> 00:54:42.950
I'll close with these, but I began with very sophisticated technology,

00:54:42.950 --> 00:54:48.255
fMRI to going to vegetative state patients and preverbal infants and so on,

00:54:48.255 --> 00:54:50.220
and how these can help us,

00:54:50.220 --> 00:54:52.870
give us information to make very difficult decisions.

00:54:52.870 --> 00:54:54.340
Then I went on to say, well,

00:54:54.340 --> 00:54:55.840
but this isn't the majority of physicians.

00:54:55.840 --> 00:54:58.140
Of course, the decisions happen just very rapidly,

00:54:58.140 --> 00:54:59.925
fast and foolish decisions,

00:54:59.925 --> 00:55:02.215
and we make all this sort of errors.

00:55:02.215 --> 00:55:06.045
In the end, instead of going skeptic on this direction,

00:55:06.045 --> 00:55:07.780
which I think we've been very skeptic,

00:55:07.780 --> 00:55:09.585
I wanted to give up a note,

00:55:09.585 --> 00:55:14.695
not from faked optimism but data that we've been gathering for many years,

00:55:14.695 --> 00:55:17.440
that I think should give us at least the idea that

00:55:17.440 --> 00:55:20.450
this pessimism of the incapacity of conversations to

00:55:20.450 --> 00:55:23.320
solve situation comes from the fact that typically we

00:55:23.320 --> 00:55:26.605
have them in the wrong setups and not where they should be.

00:55:26.605 --> 00:55:29.170
With this, I thank you very much and I'll pick any of

00:55:29.170 --> 00:55:32.335
the questions that you may want an effect. Thank you very much.

00:55:32.335 --> 00:55:34.720
Thank you very much, Mariano,

00:55:34.720 --> 00:55:37.450
for that fantastic talk.

00:55:37.450 --> 00:55:40.515
I have lots of questions myself,

00:55:40.515 --> 00:55:44.090
but we open up to the audience.

00:55:44.090 --> 00:55:48.265
Please feel free to type your questions in the Q&amp;A.

00:55:48.265 --> 00:55:51.105
As people get ready,

00:55:51.105 --> 00:55:55.570
if I may, I'd like to ask the first question.

00:55:55.570 --> 00:55:59.230
I really, really like your point about how some of

00:55:59.230 --> 00:56:04.190
these conversations are happening in the wrong spaces and that we

00:56:04.190 --> 00:56:12.775
need to find smaller groups where more malleable opinions can actually be heard.

00:56:12.775 --> 00:56:14.980
What I was curious, from what you say,

00:56:14.980 --> 00:56:19.300
it sounds like the success in this convergence and

00:56:19.300 --> 00:56:26.620
consensus making requires a few persuasive mediators who can talk to both sides.

00:56:26.620 --> 00:56:30.400
How do we inculcate such a temperament?

00:56:30.400 --> 00:56:38.895
How do we get people to have that frame with which they see issues?

00:56:38.895 --> 00:56:43.360
Do you think in educational systems we can do something,

00:56:43.360 --> 00:56:46.845
or as a society, or is it really an individual?

00:56:46.845 --> 00:56:49.865
Yeah. These, I think, are very good,

00:56:49.865 --> 00:56:51.740
and from my view,

00:56:51.740 --> 00:56:52.900
at least, very relevant question.

00:56:52.900 --> 00:56:56.070
It's been very highly studied,

00:56:57.270 --> 00:57:03.255
mostly, as you bring in your intuition in educational setups.

00:57:03.255 --> 00:57:07.030
This is typically referred as malleable minds,

00:57:07.030 --> 00:57:09.580
or as growth minds is the typical term.

00:57:09.580 --> 00:57:13.525
If you were to look in Google Scholar or for references,

00:57:13.525 --> 00:57:17.665
the right term would be a mind of growth or growth mind.

00:57:17.665 --> 00:57:19.590
The typical problem is,

00:57:19.590 --> 00:57:22.480
it's like you give a 10-year-old children a problem

00:57:22.480 --> 00:57:25.845
that's just above what they can do so that they can not solve it.

00:57:25.845 --> 00:57:27.295
That's typical of science.

00:57:27.295 --> 00:57:32.620
In science, we give problems that are just above from what we can do. Children split.

00:57:32.620 --> 00:57:34.005
Of course, it's continuous,

00:57:34.005 --> 00:57:37.495
but they typically split in two modes of reaction.

00:57:37.495 --> 00:57:39.255
It's not a completely categorical split,

00:57:39.255 --> 00:57:41.635
but there are like two tendencies.

00:57:41.635 --> 00:57:45.885
One is frustrations and like I cannot do it.

00:57:45.885 --> 00:57:51.070
Its too hard. This leads to a whole lot of behaviors.

00:57:51.070 --> 00:57:56.320
They cheat, they blame others for giving them things that they cannot do,

00:57:56.320 --> 00:57:59.080
so they build anger and so on.

00:57:59.080 --> 00:58:00.685
Other children, naturally,

00:58:00.685 --> 00:58:02.060
and those things vary,

00:58:02.060 --> 00:58:05.370
as there are variances in height and eye color and so on,

00:58:05.370 --> 00:58:07.605
there is variance also in their disposition,

00:58:07.605 --> 00:58:10.215
other children understand that they cannot do it,

00:58:10.215 --> 00:58:13.560
but understand that they can get close to it.

00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:18.315
That's why it's a mindset about growth because they understand that they

00:58:18.315 --> 00:58:23.070
can do something to break this leap between where they are and where they have to be.

00:58:23.070 --> 00:58:27.370
These stretches them. Same problem.

00:58:27.370 --> 00:58:29.625
Two very different trajectories.

00:58:29.625 --> 00:58:32.520
Once again, when this type of inhibition setups,

00:58:32.520 --> 00:58:36.630
is that this ends up being very decisive in educational trajectory,

00:58:36.630 --> 00:58:38.985
so children that do have this mindsets,

00:58:38.985 --> 00:58:41.370
they persist in them,

00:58:41.370 --> 00:58:42.990
and they start also to buy food cake.

00:58:42.990 --> 00:58:45.630
Because they start being outsiders,

00:58:45.630 --> 00:58:47.400
and the ones that do not pass the exams,

00:58:47.400 --> 00:58:50.040
and the ones that start believing that they cannot do things,

00:58:50.040 --> 00:58:51.630
and they've been longer and so on.

00:58:51.630 --> 00:58:54.930
While the other ones are the ones that end up going to the good schools and so on.

00:58:54.930 --> 00:58:57.525
Now, the good news,

00:58:57.525 --> 00:59:00.210
and that's the response to your question,

00:59:00.210 --> 00:59:02.340
is that these things can be changed.

00:59:02.340 --> 00:59:05.920
It's not easy, but it's also not hard.

00:59:06.080 --> 00:59:10.005
It's not so easy, I mean it's not just the disposition.

00:59:10.005 --> 00:59:11.940
It's not just like saying,'' I want to have

00:59:11.940 --> 00:59:15.510
an open mindset and then bloom next day, you will have it.''

00:59:15.510 --> 00:59:17.250
We have these cognitive illusions,

00:59:17.250 --> 00:59:19.230
but this is not true for any form of learning.

00:59:19.230 --> 00:59:22.995
It's not like I want to play tennis and next day you play tennis. You have to train it.

00:59:22.995 --> 00:59:27.105
In the same way, if you want to have an open mindset, you have to train it.

00:59:27.105 --> 00:59:28.890
But it's much easier than playing tennis.

00:59:28.890 --> 00:59:35.640
That's the good news. There are many different ways that have been developed,

00:59:35.640 --> 00:59:37.980
once again, in most educational setups.

00:59:37.980 --> 00:59:43.750
It's been proved that if children in time, you change them,

00:59:43.750 --> 00:59:47.930
a fixed mindset to an open or to a growth mindset,

00:59:47.930 --> 00:59:54.300
then they are in time to have a better likelihood.

00:59:54.300 --> 00:59:55.770
Those things of course, are always probabilistic,

00:59:55.770 --> 01:00:03.780
but they have a higher probability of going through a much better educational trajectory.

01:00:03.780 --> 01:00:09.045
Thank you. It sounds like this has to be instilled as an aspiration,

01:00:09.045 --> 01:00:11.325
even for the kids that don't

01:00:11.325 --> 01:00:13.620
naturally have that temperament. That this what [OVERLAPPING].

01:00:13.620 --> 01:00:15.255
Yeah it has to be. You have to,

01:00:15.255 --> 01:00:16.890
and this is training.

01:00:16.890 --> 01:00:26.130
It's not something that you have to learn,

01:00:26.130 --> 01:00:28.335
because some people have it granted,

01:00:28.335 --> 01:00:29.895
and other people don't.

01:00:29.895 --> 01:00:32.400
It's not like there is a default for these.

01:00:32.400 --> 01:00:34.845
Then you can go back on, why this happens.

01:00:34.845 --> 01:00:37.515
Some of these things may be genetic and so on,

01:00:37.515 --> 01:00:40.830
but some of these are also very early implicit training.

01:00:40.830 --> 01:00:46.290
Parents building these mentality not in ways that they understand.

01:00:46.290 --> 01:00:51.730
Sometimes completely against their will and their desire.

01:00:51.950 --> 01:00:54.715
There are different things.

01:00:54.715 --> 01:00:56.675
This is a very complicated iterative,

01:00:56.675 --> 01:01:01.310
it's not like there's a simple way on how to build this mentality.

01:01:01.310 --> 01:01:03.575
I think I'm pointing you to the idea,

01:01:03.575 --> 01:01:05.960
and it requires study.

01:01:05.960 --> 01:01:09.740
I think it would not be fair if I say I can summarize this in two minutes.

01:01:09.740 --> 01:01:12.485
But some ideas we can put in a table.

01:01:12.485 --> 01:01:15.525
One that has been known pedagogy for a long time,

01:01:15.525 --> 01:01:22.390
is people get frustrated when they have aspirations which are ridiculously high.

01:01:22.550 --> 01:01:28.290
Different educational setups work differently in this condition.

01:01:28.290 --> 01:01:30.150
One condition is you're always kind,

01:01:30.150 --> 01:01:37.110
and there's a very famous pedagogical idea by Lev Vygotsky,

01:01:37.110 --> 01:01:43.500
the Russian pedagogue do which is the disown of proximity.

01:01:43.500 --> 01:01:47.820
It's the idea that learning has to be just above.

01:01:47.820 --> 01:01:50.340
But just above in these problems that I told you,

01:01:50.340 --> 01:01:53.805
they are not just above, they're like a significant step above.

01:01:53.805 --> 01:01:55.680
Those are the ones that can frustrate you,

01:01:55.680 --> 01:02:01.515
and that's not where you should typically have children grow in their educational setup.

01:02:01.515 --> 01:02:03.615
This is one reason that will make,

01:02:03.615 --> 01:02:06.825
a lot of children make the conclusion

01:02:06.825 --> 01:02:10.500
that they cannot solve problems which are more difficult than they can afford,

01:02:10.500 --> 01:02:13.920
because they've given problems that they really could not solve because they were so

01:02:13.920 --> 01:02:15.600
difficult than they have made

01:02:15.600 --> 01:02:19.275
this conclusion because of a wrong sampling history of problems.

01:02:19.275 --> 01:02:22.680
This is just one idea of how you can

01:02:22.680 --> 01:02:26.430
begin biasing falling in one side of the camp or the other one.

01:02:26.430 --> 01:02:29.610
It's complex, but they're substantially territory.

01:02:29.610 --> 01:02:34.590
I think that it's one of the cases in which we can make informed policies,

01:02:34.590 --> 01:02:37.170
both at large public policies,

01:02:37.170 --> 01:02:39.270
but also in parental policies,

01:02:39.270 --> 01:02:42.450
like how to grow our Children in a better way.

01:02:42.450 --> 01:02:46.170
Thanks, Mariano. It also sounds like this is the thing that you would have to

01:02:46.170 --> 01:02:49.770
persistently reinforces when you would have to keep reinforcing.

01:02:49.770 --> 01:02:54.405
Yeah, that's why I'm making it always point it's complex.

01:02:54.405 --> 01:02:56.085
It's also domain-specific.

01:02:56.085 --> 01:03:00.900
Some children, they may have domains in which they have this mentality,

01:03:00.900 --> 01:03:02.355
and they know that consult them,

01:03:02.355 --> 01:03:05.080
but they are bad in Maths.

01:03:05.420 --> 01:03:07.530
As I was saying, intuition,

01:03:07.530 --> 01:03:09.750
it's not like there's people with good intuition,

01:03:09.750 --> 01:03:13.755
people have different ways of relating with

01:03:13.755 --> 01:03:18.090
adversity and with resilience in different domains.

01:03:18.090 --> 01:03:22.270
One thing which is also very important to know is that,

01:03:23.180 --> 01:03:25.515
and this comes from

01:03:25.515 --> 01:03:27.970
a very basic idea

01:03:30.200 --> 01:03:36.300
I made the case that we make very rapid conclusions which we trust a lot.

01:03:36.300 --> 01:03:40.140
But it's even worse than that because then these conclusions begin

01:03:40.140 --> 01:03:44.280
changing us in a way that we confirm the conclusions that we've made.

01:03:44.280 --> 01:03:48.480
This happens individually but also socially because we tend to look

01:03:48.480 --> 01:03:52.935
for data that confirms the hypothesis that we have already made.

01:03:52.935 --> 01:03:56.655
Imagine you're in a point of bifurcation where you can make the claim,

01:03:56.655 --> 01:03:58.545
I'm bad at Math or I'm good at Math,

01:03:58.545 --> 01:04:01.035
and you're right there, and you'll make your claim.

01:04:01.035 --> 01:04:03.885
It's like a bit like measurements in quantum physics.

01:04:03.885 --> 01:04:07.995
Once you've made this call and you say I am bad at that,

01:04:07.995 --> 01:04:10.420
then you've changed reality,

01:04:10.460 --> 01:04:13.410
because once you've made this call,

01:04:13.410 --> 01:04:19.410
this is what I like George Soros, the finance.

01:04:19.410 --> 01:04:25.680
He started with Opera and he knows a lot of human behavior,

01:04:25.680 --> 01:04:27.645
and this is why he's made a lot of money

01:04:27.645 --> 01:04:30.585
because he understands how people behave in a very deep way.

01:04:30.585 --> 01:04:32.700
It's interesting because the way I think he thinks of

01:04:32.700 --> 01:04:35.625
the stock market as the best laboratory of human behavior,

01:04:35.625 --> 01:04:39.165
and he keeps them coming [inaudible] this idea which he calls reflexivity.

01:04:39.165 --> 01:04:41.325
It's not a new idea, it's an old idea and it's very powerful.

01:04:41.325 --> 01:04:43.875
He says that this is one of the central ideas in the market.

01:04:43.875 --> 01:04:47.790
The market makes a belief that an action is going,

01:04:47.790 --> 01:04:49.230
the stock is going to grow,

01:04:49.230 --> 01:04:52.320
and this decision that the market has made changes

01:04:52.320 --> 01:04:55.440
reality and the way that now this stock changes,

01:04:55.440 --> 01:04:58.590
because people will be consistent with the idea that they've made.

01:04:58.590 --> 01:05:00.390
This leads to financial bubbles.

01:05:00.390 --> 01:05:02.940
There is a moment in which there is an inconsistency

01:05:02.940 --> 01:05:07.200
between people's beliefs and reality,

01:05:07.200 --> 01:05:08.775
and then there's an explosion.

01:05:08.775 --> 01:05:13.080
The idea we'd like to bring here is that we also have the psychological bubbles.

01:05:13.080 --> 01:05:15.660
We tell ourselves a story,

01:05:15.660 --> 01:05:18.675
I'm good at this or I'm bad at this and the story builds,

01:05:18.675 --> 01:05:22.185
because we sampled data in a way that we look for consistency on that.

01:05:22.185 --> 01:05:24.480
Eventually, sometimes it also blows,

01:05:24.480 --> 01:05:27.660
but for a long time it gives us the ability of having

01:05:27.660 --> 01:05:31.620
completely different mentalities. This is also important.

01:05:31.620 --> 01:05:33.270
Sometimes parents, they are not also aware,

01:05:33.270 --> 01:05:36.060
like sometimes statements are very heavy,

01:05:36.060 --> 01:05:38.560
like saying he's not good at Math.

01:05:38.810 --> 01:05:41.220
I'll tell you one that happens to me,

01:05:41.220 --> 01:05:43.575
my son he's the one that tends to.

01:05:43.575 --> 01:05:48.060
He falls a lot. This is not a typical behavior.

01:05:48.060 --> 01:05:52.440
If they go to up to a birthday party and one falls,

01:05:52.440 --> 01:05:54.105
and has ache,

01:05:54.105 --> 01:05:58.840
typically it's my son because he's not the best control and so on.

01:06:01.940 --> 01:06:04.140
I'm very versed on these,

01:06:04.140 --> 01:06:09.660
but sometimes I make the error of making a caricature of this,

01:06:09.660 --> 01:06:12.135
making fun of this and making, once again.

01:06:12.135 --> 01:06:20.310
I know here it tells me that I should never do this because the only thing I'm doing is,

01:06:20.310 --> 01:06:26.235
I'm fueling more something that actually begun being self-consistent.

01:06:26.235 --> 01:06:28.570
What I'm saying with this is that

01:06:28.610 --> 01:06:33.450
sometimes they are very strong biases that are difficult to eradicate.

01:06:33.450 --> 01:06:37.995
They take like substantial attentional control

01:06:37.995 --> 01:06:43.740
not to fall onto these errors that parents often fall to stigmatize even their children,

01:06:43.740 --> 01:06:45.825
which is the last thing they would want to do on Earth,

01:06:45.825 --> 01:06:48.969
but sometimes we do, nevertheless.

01:06:50.960 --> 01:06:54.960
Really insightful. Thank you so much, Mariano.

01:06:54.960 --> 01:06:56.970
Going back to something earlier in

01:06:56.970 --> 01:07:00.614
your presentation from one of our frequent participants,

01:07:00.614 --> 01:07:06.465
Jenny, she asks, is bouba kiki correspondence a form of synesthesia?

01:07:06.465 --> 01:07:12.430
If so does this tell us that everybody is capable of developing synesthesia?

01:07:12.800 --> 01:07:18.045
Yes, it is. For those that may not be familiar with the word,

01:07:18.045 --> 01:07:26.200
synesthesia means that there's automatic relation between different sensory modalities.

01:07:26.620 --> 01:07:30.840
Some people, when they see some shapes,

01:07:30.850 --> 01:07:35.540
as we saw here, they believe that they correspond to certain sounds.

01:07:35.540 --> 01:07:40.124
But there's also some people that when they perceive certain tastes,

01:07:40.124 --> 01:07:45.090
these tastes produce certain sounds or they relate to certain shapes.

01:07:45.090 --> 01:07:48.600
So vision, audition, taste, smell,

01:07:48.600 --> 01:07:52.920
which normally are independent modalities, they're automatically linked.

01:07:52.920 --> 01:07:57.824
What this experiment shows is that we all have certain forms of synesthesia.

01:07:57.824 --> 01:07:59.790
Some of those are cultural, for instance.

01:07:59.790 --> 01:08:02.565
Another example would be color and temperature.

01:08:02.565 --> 01:08:06.765
Most of us think those things a priori are independent but they are not,

01:08:06.765 --> 01:08:09.465
and the reason they're not is because their material corresponded,

01:08:09.465 --> 01:08:11.655
which is the majority of materials when they are

01:08:11.655 --> 01:08:15.600
incandescent and hence they're warm, they're red.

01:08:15.600 --> 01:08:18.720
We tend to think that red is hot and that blue,

01:08:18.720 --> 01:08:23.310
which is more the color of eyes, is cold,

01:08:23.310 --> 01:08:26.610
and this is a form of cultural synesthesia,

01:08:26.610 --> 01:08:33.930
which is driven by material experience like the one I showed with the mouth.

01:08:33.930 --> 01:08:36.000
Another example which I really like,

01:08:36.000 --> 01:08:39.705
and it's in my book Priya which you mentioned,

01:08:39.705 --> 01:08:41.385
so you may have read about it,

01:08:41.385 --> 01:08:47.744
is the correspondence between time and space which a priori are different entities,

01:08:47.744 --> 01:08:50.310
but the majority of the people, and if you think like,

01:08:50.310 --> 01:08:54.885
how we use language is you feel like Christmas is approaching.

01:08:54.885 --> 01:08:56.160
Is approaching from where?

01:08:56.160 --> 01:08:57.840
Like Christmas is in time,

01:08:57.840 --> 01:09:00.195
but we have the feeling that it's ahead of us.

01:09:00.195 --> 01:09:02.610
If you choose ahead and the past is behind,

01:09:02.610 --> 01:09:05.910
this seems like that's just how it has to be like Kiki and Buddha.

01:09:05.910 --> 01:09:07.290
But there are other cultures,

01:09:07.290 --> 01:09:11.190
I mentioned example of the Imanas which is a culture

01:09:11.190 --> 01:09:15.510
in the northwest of Argentina and Bolivia,

01:09:15.510 --> 01:09:17.700
and Chile, and this culture has,

01:09:17.700 --> 01:09:21.845
in their language, the word for in front also means past.

01:09:21.845 --> 01:09:26.615
It's the same word and the word for behind which is naida also means in the front.

01:09:26.615 --> 01:09:29.135
It's very interesting because they might as, what they say.

01:09:29.135 --> 01:09:34.210
Is that the reason the past is in front is because it's the only thing we know,

01:09:34.210 --> 01:09:39.210
and there's this relation between when you want to tell someone,

01:09:39.210 --> 01:09:40.380
"Did you understand someone?"

01:09:40.380 --> 01:09:41.820
You say, "You see."

01:09:41.820 --> 01:09:45.585
Like seeing is understanding, seeing is knowing.

01:09:45.585 --> 01:09:47.579
If you use this relation,

01:09:47.579 --> 01:09:49.470
the only thing you see, the only thing you know,

01:09:49.470 --> 01:09:51.150
the only thing you understand is the past,

01:09:51.150 --> 01:09:52.920
and the things that you do not see,

01:09:52.920 --> 01:09:54.600
you do not understand and you do not know is

01:09:54.600 --> 01:09:57.555
the future with hands is behind, it's in the back.

01:09:57.555 --> 01:10:02.190
It's very interesting because we believe that this correspondence,

01:10:02.190 --> 01:10:05.490
the steady correspondence between time and space seems very logical,

01:10:05.490 --> 01:10:08.595
like the future, we advance to the future that's how it should be.

01:10:08.595 --> 01:10:10.260
But someone comes in a different culture

01:10:10.260 --> 01:10:12.075
then you know what for me it's the other way around.

01:10:12.075 --> 01:10:14.310
There are many other ones like the Aztecs,

01:10:14.310 --> 01:10:15.885
they have circular type.

01:10:15.885 --> 01:10:18.780
Now it's not only different correspondence between what's ahead and

01:10:18.780 --> 01:10:21.525
what's behind but also in the topology of space.

01:10:21.525 --> 01:10:26.280
Interestingly, there's also not complete freedom because at all cultures

01:10:26.280 --> 01:10:32.070
known use one-dimensional representation for time,

01:10:32.070 --> 01:10:34.470
and that's very interesting because it seems to be

01:10:34.470 --> 01:10:37.245
as far as we can tell from the way we perceive time,

01:10:37.245 --> 01:10:42.255
that this is a physical limitation of how we can organize a certain space.

01:10:42.255 --> 01:10:45.300
So there are many examples of synesthesia.

01:10:45.300 --> 01:10:49.455
Also very interesting just to tell an anecdote I think maybe because Edward Hubbard,

01:10:49.455 --> 01:10:51.900
and Ramachandra, which I mentioned in their paper,

01:10:51.900 --> 01:11:00.090
they were doing a lot of the interesting work of the neuroscience of anesthesia.

01:11:00.090 --> 01:11:04.410
Edward Hubbard, we did some work together and we're very good friends,

01:11:04.410 --> 01:11:10.425
and the problem with working with synesthetics is that it has this flare of genius,

01:11:10.425 --> 01:11:17.685
so musicians, they claim that they see music and this gives brilliancy,

01:11:17.685 --> 01:11:20.985
or mathematicians, a great mathematician will tell you,

01:11:20.985 --> 01:11:23.650
"I can hear the sound of Pi."

01:11:24.850 --> 01:11:29.040
I'm not just like the product of work,

01:11:29.040 --> 01:11:31.920
I'm a product of genius because I got like these very sophisticated.

01:11:31.920 --> 01:11:33.570
So it gives like a sense of poetry,

01:11:33.570 --> 01:11:34.980
so people lie on this.

01:11:34.980 --> 01:11:36.630
You get a lot of people that come to

01:11:36.630 --> 01:11:39.450
the laboratory saying that they are synesthetic which they are not.

01:11:39.450 --> 01:11:46.110
There are interesting tests to measure whether someone is genuinely synesthetic or not.

01:11:46.110 --> 01:11:51.480
For instance, one is typical synesthesia is people see letters of different colors.

01:11:51.480 --> 01:11:55.980
So like Ls will be blue and As will be red.

01:11:55.980 --> 01:11:58.470
You say, perfect. So now do the

01:11:58.470 --> 01:12:03.085
following and you give them a maze which is full of Ls and there's one A,

01:12:03.085 --> 01:12:06.740
and if you're not synesthetic this is very hard because it requires a lot of attention,

01:12:06.740 --> 01:12:08.450
but if it's red on green,

01:12:08.450 --> 01:12:10.735
then this just pops out and it's very rapid.

01:12:10.735 --> 01:12:13.290
Some people are real synesthetics and these people,

01:12:13.290 --> 01:12:15.780
they just can find it in a fraction of a

01:12:15.780 --> 01:12:19.170
second independently of how many destructors there and so on,

01:12:19.170 --> 01:12:23.235
while some people claim they're synesthetics but in truth they are not.

01:12:23.235 --> 01:12:27.600
There are many more interesting stories about synesthesia but we could go a very long.

01:12:27.600 --> 01:12:29.130
That's fantastic.

