01:28:21.060 --> 01:28:23.730
and more complexity in the distributions,

01:28:23.730 --> 01:28:32.715
and more lack of understanding and clear definitions that builds this incompatibility.

01:28:32.715 --> 01:28:34.950
This may be, sorry, a bit of a long answer,

01:28:34.950 --> 01:28:38.700
but if it goes down to say that I think

01:28:38.700 --> 01:28:43.695
that part of very big discussions between these fields.

01:28:43.695 --> 01:28:46.215
In the end are because we do not have

01:28:46.215 --> 01:28:51.225
yet mature and good definitions of what an emotion is,

01:28:51.225 --> 01:28:52.620
of what sadness is,

01:28:52.620 --> 01:28:55.570
of what emotional intelligence is.

01:28:55.610 --> 01:28:59.865
Whatever attempt someone makes to do it,

01:28:59.865 --> 01:29:05.625
like the examples you are making or like Ekman and all the famous examples,

01:29:05.625 --> 01:29:08.700
are prone to failure like all science.

01:29:08.700 --> 01:29:10.560
I myself, I'm very sympathetic,

01:29:10.560 --> 01:29:11.985
because I think they need to be done,

01:29:11.985 --> 01:29:15.525
and it's an approximation and they will fail like all scientific models have failed,

01:29:15.525 --> 01:29:22.935
this one are more likely to fail again because the territory is highly variable.

01:29:22.935 --> 01:29:26.535
I think we have to pursue this direction,

01:29:26.535 --> 01:29:28.470
but not stubbornly,

01:29:28.470 --> 01:29:32.175
but understanding that as all scientific model,

01:29:32.175 --> 01:29:34.125
is the best approximation that we have,

01:29:34.125 --> 01:29:36.825
it's the best that we can do with the data we have.

01:29:36.825 --> 01:29:39.060
We hold it, We have to be open,

01:29:39.060 --> 01:29:40.605
and once again malleable,

01:29:40.605 --> 01:29:44.010
and understand that we have not to stick religiously to

01:29:44.010 --> 01:29:47.655
it because it's our baby model and the one we constructed.

01:29:47.655 --> 01:29:52.020
But in the moment that it does not hold to the evidence

01:29:52.020 --> 01:29:54.090
and there's sufficient contradiction that we need

01:29:54.090 --> 01:29:56.950
a better model than its time eventually to change.

01:29:56.950 --> 01:29:59.945
I think you've captured very nicely

01:29:59.945 --> 01:30:03.815
the provisional nature of knowledge and science, basically.

01:30:03.815 --> 01:30:07.445
What you know at any given time is the best to date

01:30:07.445 --> 01:30:12.020
and has limitations and you have to be aware of the limitations.

01:30:12.020 --> 01:30:15.665
Therefore you have to be willing to change your mind as you get better data,

01:30:15.665 --> 01:30:17.490
more evidence as you said,

01:30:17.490 --> 01:30:20.910
you have to build a more refined conceptual model.

01:30:20.910 --> 01:30:22.890
I could keep going on,

01:30:22.890 --> 01:30:24.300
I have so many more questions,

01:30:24.300 --> 01:30:27.705
but I think we need to close with one final question,

01:30:27.705 --> 01:30:31.755
which is Jenny Wagner asking again,

01:30:31.755 --> 01:30:34.619
"Following up on the bifurcation of decisions,

01:30:34.619 --> 01:30:40.020
is there any neurological reason why any change of decision after

01:30:40.020 --> 01:30:46.004
the first one has been taken happens more often abruptly instead of slowly.

01:30:46.004 --> 01:30:49.260
For example, going suddenly feeling,

01:30:49.260 --> 01:30:51.390
being good at math's after all,

01:30:51.390 --> 01:30:54.820
when having solved the one difficult problem?"

01:30:56.030 --> 01:31:02.850
I'm not sure. I saw two different answers to this question,

01:31:02.850 --> 01:31:05.430
I'm not sure either of them except one,

01:31:05.430 --> 01:31:08.880
but so one thing is because you

01:31:08.880 --> 01:31:13.185
mentioned the brain evidence that so far I've been speaking about psychological evidence.

01:31:13.185 --> 01:31:18.720
There's a lot of neural evidence for all what I've been describing,

01:31:18.720 --> 01:31:23.565
and this comes from a way that a lot of

01:31:23.565 --> 01:31:29.490
neuroscience has changed in the last years in how decision-making progresses,

01:31:29.490 --> 01:31:33.780
which is on a highly variation strategy.

01:31:33.780 --> 01:31:37.935
There are many demonstrations [inaudible], for instance,

01:31:37.935 --> 01:31:42.270
in Geneve would be I think that the best person to look for this information,

01:31:42.270 --> 01:31:46.275
showing that you can account for a lot of strange things and decision-making.

01:31:46.275 --> 01:31:51.345
If you look at the neural circuits as doing Bayesian computations.

01:31:51.345 --> 01:31:54.795
This is the essence of the self-fulfilling prophecy,

01:31:54.795 --> 01:31:57.795
because you make a prior and once you make a prior,

01:31:57.795 --> 01:32:04.990
you sample evidence prior as you exactly describing it in an unbalanced manner.

01:32:05.150 --> 01:32:10.109
There is neural evidence showing that once you've committed to a decision,

01:32:10.109 --> 01:32:14.550
then neural circuits change on a way that they decide differently.

01:32:14.550 --> 01:32:16.140
It's not a magical construction,

01:32:16.140 --> 01:32:19.920
this is implemented in a way that we understand in neurons.

01:32:19.920 --> 01:32:22.440
The second thing is this idea that there's

01:32:22.440 --> 01:32:26.520
a roll of all these decisions being much faster.

01:32:26.520 --> 01:32:28.860
At least what comes to mind to me,

01:32:28.860 --> 01:32:32.500
I'm not sure this is exactly the answer you're looking for,

01:32:32.810 --> 01:32:39.855
people have studied this very nicely in the history of wall sport records.

01:32:39.855 --> 01:32:44.175
There are many records that have not been broken for years and years.

01:32:44.175 --> 01:32:46.785
Then eventually they're broken, once they're broken,

01:32:46.785 --> 01:32:50.700
there is a very rapid repetition,

01:32:50.700 --> 01:32:55.800
the cleanest example is running one mile in less than four minutes.

01:32:55.800 --> 01:32:59.835
I think that's which was no one could do it,

01:32:59.835 --> 01:33:01.320
then 40 years ago,

01:33:01.320 --> 01:33:04.110
somebody did that, and since then there was like 1,400 people,

01:33:04.110 --> 01:33:05.370
it had happened already,

01:33:05.370 --> 01:33:07.365
but before that for 40 years, no one did it.

01:33:07.365 --> 01:33:09.090
As of course there's an issue of technology,

01:33:09.090 --> 01:33:12.570
but it's not only technology because it's really this game changer,

01:33:12.570 --> 01:33:15.810
this bifurcation and the explanation for these,

01:33:15.810 --> 01:33:19.065
once again, it's only the force of

01:33:19.065 --> 01:33:24.480
our own minds to convince ourselves of what we can do and what we cannot do.

01:33:24.480 --> 01:33:26.115
I would like to tamper this,

01:33:26.115 --> 01:33:27.525
because it's not like I say,

01:33:27.525 --> 01:33:29.340
I want to fly and then I fly.

01:33:29.340 --> 01:33:32.430
This is by no means, it's obvious,

01:33:32.430 --> 01:33:35.190
but I want to state I'm not

01:33:35.190 --> 01:33:38.775
predicating here that dream about something then it would come true.

01:33:38.775 --> 01:33:41.970
This is not the case, people live in very bad realities,

01:33:41.970 --> 01:33:44.040
and they struggle,

01:33:44.040 --> 01:33:46.380
and they cannot do better than they do.

01:33:46.380 --> 01:33:48.810
But there's, this said,

01:33:48.810 --> 01:33:53.700
there's much more margin than we think for the range of beliefs,

01:33:53.700 --> 01:33:58.725
for the reasons I just explained in our own performances and in our own decisions.

01:33:58.725 --> 01:34:01.950
On a way that once we understand that something is doable,

01:34:01.950 --> 01:34:05.820
they believe that it could not be done is eradicated,

01:34:05.820 --> 01:34:10.530
then this is a game changer that then builds on a rapid cascade.

01:34:10.530 --> 01:34:14.070
This is an example from social sciences, so to say.

01:34:14.070 --> 01:34:18.820
But I think it's the best example for the psychological question that you were asking.

01:34:19.310 --> 01:34:22.245
Thank you so much.

01:34:22.245 --> 01:34:26.100
There's absolutely fascinating talk and thank you so much for taking

01:34:26.100 --> 01:34:30.825
the time to thoughtfully answer all these questions.

01:34:30.825 --> 01:34:36.960
I really should draw us to a close because we are over our time.

01:34:36.960 --> 01:34:43.005
Thank you so much Mariano once again for coming and giving us this wonderful talk.

01:34:43.005 --> 01:34:48.540
Very thought-provoking and hopefully we all leave here aspiring to have as

01:34:48.540 --> 01:34:54.345
malleable a mind as possible to make our ways in the world.

01:34:54.345 --> 01:34:55.905
Thank you so much.

01:34:55.905 --> 01:35:00.945
Let's give Mariano a really round of applause virtual applause.

01:35:00.945 --> 01:35:02.370
Yes. Thank you.

01:35:02.370 --> 01:35:04.065
Thank you so much Priya.

01:35:04.065 --> 01:35:05.790
Thank you Mariano.

01:35:05.790 --> 01:35:07.360
Thank you.

